window.FALLAKTE = {"register":"fallakte","generated":"2026-10-11","cases":[{"slug":"polycythaemia-vera","id":"FND-C-0001","disease":"polycythaemia-vera","disease_id":"FND-D-0145","name":"polycythaemia vera","common":"a blood cancer","opened":"2026-10-10","updated":"2026-10-11","status":"active","one_line":"A blood cancer with one known driver mutation, a treatment that lowers a number without touching the cause, and a natural history nobody can predict.","working_with":[{"kind":"nonprofit","named":false}],"obstacles":["who-progresses"],"models":[{"tool":"Strangschrift","ref":"../../../../Strangschrift/examples/jak2-sites.strang","name":"jak2-sites","access":"repo","live":null},{"tool":"Strangschrift","ref":"../../../../Strangschrift/examples/stat5-target.strang","name":"stat5-target","access":"repo","live":null},{"tool":"Schaltwerk","ref":"../../../../Schaltwerk/examples/jak2-brake.schalt","name":"jak2-brake","access":"private","live":null},{"tool":"Schaltwerk","ref":"../../../../Schaltwerk/examples/jak2-v617f.schalt","name":"jak2-v617f","access":"private","live":null},{"tool":"Schaltwerk","ref":"../../../../Schaltwerk/examples/jak2-stat5.schalt","name":"jak2-stat5","access":"private","live":null},{"tool":"Schaltwerk","ref":"../../../../Schaltwerk/examples/jak2-off.schalt","name":"jak2-off","access":"private","live":null},{"tool":"Schaltwerk","ref":"../../../../Schaltwerk/examples/pfcp.schalt","name":"pfcp","access":"private","live":null},{"tool":"Strombild","ref":"../../../../strombild/examples/erythropoiesis.strombild","name":"erythropoiesis","access":"repo","live":null},{"tool":"Strombild","ref":"../../../../strombild/examples/erythropoiesis-pv.strombild","name":"erythropoiesis-pv","access":"repo","live":null},{"tool":"Strombild","ref":"../../../../strombild/examples/clonal-escape.strombild","name":"clonal-escape","access":"repo","live":null},{"tool":"Strombild","ref":"../../../../strombild/examples/iron.strombild","name":"iron","access":"repo","live":null}],"openable":0,"questions":[{"id":"Q1","status":"open","obstacles":["who-progresses"]},{"id":"Q2","status":"partial","obstacles":["who-progresses"]},{"id":"Q3","status":"open","obstacles":["who-progresses"]},{"id":"Q4","status":"open","obstacles":[]},{"id":"Q5","status":"open","obstacles":["who-progresses"]},{"id":"Q6","status":"open","obstacles":[]},{"id":"Q7","status":"open","obstacles":["who-progresses"]},{"id":"Q8","status":"open","obstacles":[]},{"id":"Q9","status":"open","obstacles":[]}],"open_questions":9,"record":{"capability":"managed","delivery":"partly","mechanism":"established","intervention":"suppressive","blockers":["cost","diagnosis","evidence-incomplete","knowledge"],"reach":0.3},"doc":{"html":"<h1 id=\"polycythaemia-vera-case-file\">Polycythaemia vera — case file</h1>\n<p><code>FND-C-0001</code> · opened 2026-10-10 · active · the disease record is <a href=\"query.html?q=slug:polycythaemia-vera\"><code>polycythaemia-vera</code></a> (<code>FND-D-0145</code>, <code>MONDO:0009891</code>)</p>\n<p><b>One mutation, found in 2005, in a gene whose job is understood, in a loop whose physiology is textbook. And the standard treatment is to take blood out.</b> That gap is why this case is open.</p>\n<p><b>Who is working on it.</b> A nonprofit brought this disease to the register as its first case; they are not named here until they have agreed to appear in a public repository, which is why <code>case.yaml</code> records <code>named: false</code> rather than a blank. The models and the mistakes are ours.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"1-where-it-stands\">1. Where it stands</h2>\n<p>Read off the record, not re-rated here.</p>\n<table><tr><th style=\"text-align:left\"></th><th style=\"text-align:left\"></th></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">mechanism</td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><code>established</code>, with four named holes</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">intervention</td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><code>suppressive</code> — near-normal life, treated indefinitely</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">capability / delivery</td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><b>managed</b>, and reach is dominated by patients never found</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">what is suppressed</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">the <b>consequence</b>. The clone is untouched by venesection and aspirin</td></tr></table>\n<p>The record&#x27;s own summary of the gap: PV is the sibling of chronic myeloid leukaemia, where a drug sits in the driver kinase and the clone shrinks until it is hard to find. Here the available kinase inhibitor is not selective for the mutant and does not do that. <b>So the disease is controlled by lowering a number, and the thing that raises the number carries on.</b></p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"2-the-mechanism-step-by-step\">2. The mechanism, step by step</h2>\n<p>One step per row. <code>established</code> means it would survive a refutation attempt. <code>partial</code> means the step is real and its terms are not settled. <code>open</code> means we are naming a gap, not describing a finding. <b>The empty cells in the last column are the list of things worth building.</b></p>\n<h3 id=\"how-certain-is-step-1\">How certain is step 1?</h3>\n<p><b>Asked directly: are we certain it is the mutation?</b> As certain as this kind of claim gets, with one precise limit.</p>\n<p>Four independent things make it causal rather than correlated. Put the mutation into a mouse and the mouse gets too many red cells, which is the experiment that settles direction. The patients who do not have V617F mostly have a <i>different</i> mutation in the <i>same gene</i>, in exon 12, and get the same disease, so two unrelated lesions converge on one phenotype through one gene. Germline mutations elsewhere in the same signalling pathway also produce too many red cells, which is the contrast case on our own board. And the effect is dose-dependent: a higher mutant fraction goes with more disease, and the highest fractions with the most thrombosis.</p>\n<p><b>The limit is that it is necessary and NOT SUFFICIENT</b>, and the numbers are blunt about it. Screening a general population with a sensitive assay finds the mutation in about one adult in thirty. The disease occurs in about one person in a hundred thousand per year. <b>The mutation is roughly three thousand times commoner than the disease.</b> So &quot;it is the mutation&quot; is true of the erythrocytosis and false as an account of who becomes ill. What is sufficient is the mutation plus the clone plus time, which is why this chain has twelve rows and not one.</p>\n<table><tr><th style=\"text-align:left\">#</th><th style=\"text-align:left\">what changes</th><th style=\"text-align:left\">status</th><th style=\"text-align:left\">where you can watch it</th></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">1</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">A single haematopoietic stem cell acquires a point mutation in <i>JAK2</i>: valine 617 becomes phenylalanine. <b>Somatic. One cell, during life.</b></td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><code>established</code></td><td style=\"text-align:left\">Strangschrift <code>jak2-sites</code></td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">1b</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">Whether this is the FIRST event. In some patients it is; in others <i>TET2</i> or <i>DNMT3A</i> arrived first, and the order appears to matter for which disease results.</td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><code>open</code> — see <b>Q8</b></td><td style=\"text-align:left\">—</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">2</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">Why that cell, that base, that person</td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><code>open</code></td><td style=\"text-align:left\">—</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">3</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">V617 sits in the pseudokinase domain, which normally holds the kinase domain shut. The substitution loosens the restraint.</td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><code>established</code> <i>that it does</i>; <code>partial</code> on <i>how</i></td><td style=\"text-align:left\">Schaltwerk <code>jak2-brake</code> → <code>jak2-v617f</code></td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">4</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">The unrestrained kinase phosphorylates the erythropoietin receptor&#x27;s tyrosines and then STAT5, <b>with no erythropoietin bound anywhere</b></td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><code>established</code></td><td style=\"text-align:left\">Schaltwerk <code>jak2-stat5</code></td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">5</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">The off switches still work. A phosphatase removes the phosphate; SOCS1 grips the kinase&#x27;s own phosphotyrosine and takes it to be destroyed. <b>The disease is not a broken off switch — it is a stuck on switch the off switch cannot keep up with.</b></td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><code>established</code></td><td style=\"text-align:left\">Schaltwerk <code>jak2-off</code></td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">6</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">Phosphorylated STAT5 pairs, enters the nucleus, and binds its motif in target promoters</td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><code>established</code> <i>that it does</i>; <code>open</code> on which targets matter here</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">Strangschrift <code>stat5-target</code>, Schaltwerk <code>transcription</code></td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">7</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">The mutant erythroid progenitor survives and matures <b>without erythropoietin</b>. This is the 1974 observation that waited thirty-one years for its cause.</td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><code>established</code></td><td style=\"text-align:left\">Strombild <code>erythropoiesis-pv</code> (the one deleted edge)</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">8</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">The mutant stem cell out-renews its neighbours and the clone grows, over <b>decades</b>, from one cell to a large fraction of the marrow</td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><code>established</code> that it happens; its magnitude is now <b>measured</b> per person; <b><code>open</code> on what the advantage is made of, and on why some clones shrink</b></td><td style=\"text-align:left\">Strombild <code>clonal-escape</code>, and see <b>Q1</b>, <b>Q2</b>, <b>Q7</b></td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">9</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">Once enough of the marrow descends from that cell, red-cell mass rises</td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><code>established</code></td><td style=\"text-align:left\">Strombild <code>clonal-escape</code></td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">9b</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">Building that extra red cell mass <b>consumes iron</b>, and the body holds only a few grams. The growth phase draws the iron stores down, which is why a substantial share of patients are already iron deficient when they are found.</td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><code>established</code></td><td style=\"text-align:left\">Strombild <code>iron</code></td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">9c</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">The over-driven marrow suppresses hepcidin, which opens the iron gate, so <b>the body increases the iron supply to the clone.</b> Iron deficiency opens the same gate further.</td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><code>established</code></td><td style=\"text-align:left\">Strombild <code>iron</code></td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">10</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">The kidney reads the extra oxygen and turns erythropoietin <b>down</b>, to a fraction of normal. The sensor is intact. It is shouting <i>enough</i> at a marrow that has stopped listening. <b>High red cells with low erythropoietin is the loop&#x27;s fingerprint, and it is the diagnostic signature.</b></td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><code>established</code></td><td style=\"text-align:left\">Strombild <code>erythropoiesis-pv</code>, <code>clonal-escape</code></td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">11</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">Viscosity rises with the haematocrit, and platelets, leukocytes and endothelium are activated too. The result is thrombosis — stroke, myocardial infarction, clots in the hepatic and portal veins. <b>This is what kills.</b></td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><code>partial</code> — lowering the haematocrit below 45% lowers the risk and does <b>not</b> abolish it</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">—</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">12</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">In a minority, the clone goes on to myelofibrosis or acute leukaemia</td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><code>open</code></td><td style=\"text-align:left\">—</td></tr></table>\n<p><b>Steps 1b, 2, 11 and 12 have no model and no mechanism.</b> Step 8 is the sharpest row in the table: we can say the clone expands, we can now put a measured number on how fast in a given person, and we cannot say what sets that number or why it sometimes reverses. The model is a precise statement of the ignorance rather than a cover for it, and on 2026-10-10 that was tested when somebody else&#x27;s data contradicted it. See section 3.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"3-what-we-got-wrong\">3. What we got wrong</h2>\n<p><b>2026-10-10 — &quot;it&#x27;s genetic, so you get bad code and a faulty protein.&quot;</b> That is the ordinary framing and it fails the obvious test. If the mutation were inherited the disease would arrive in childhood. Median age at diagnosis is about sixty.</p>\n<p>The test is worth keeping because it generalises: <b>a germline defect in a clearance or control pathway shows up early.</b> Familial hypercholesterolaemia is the clean control — a cholesterol clearance pathway broken from conception, and the homozygous form produces skin deposits in childhood and coronary disease in the teens. It was absent from Nordstern and that argument is what queued it.</p>\n<p>And the control <i>inside this very phenotype</i> is sharper still. Familial erythrocytosis is germline: the erythropoietin receptor is truncated so the site its off switch grips is simply gone. Too many red cells, from a different step, <b>and it presents in childhood.</b> Same phenotype in words. Different age of onset, because one defect was there from conception and the other arrived later. Schaltwerk&#x27;s <code>pfcp</code> is on the board beside <code>jak2-v617f</code> for exactly this reason.</p>\n<p><b>The resolution: V617F is somatic.</b> Not in the egg or the sperm, not in every cell, not passed to children.</p>\n<h3 id=\"born-with-it-is-not-the-same-as-inherited\">&quot;Born with it&quot; is not the same as &quot;inherited&quot;</h3>\n<p><b>The question that forced this section, asked 2026-10-10:</b> <i>if a child is born with the mutation, then every cell has the faulty protein, so every cell should be overproducing. How can a population of cells exist that does not have the faulty protein?</i></p>\n<p><b>Because the mutation was never in the egg.</b> Inherited means the typo is in the fertilised egg, so every cell copied from it carries the typo, and there is no clean population anywhere in the body. Somatic means the typo happens in <b>one cell of a body that is already built.</b> At thirty-three weeks of gestation a fetus is of the order of a trillion cells. One of its blood stem cells mistypes one base while copying its own DNA. <b>That cell&#x27;s descendants carry it. Nothing else in the body does, because nothing else is descended from it.</b></p>\n<p>So the clean population exists for the dullest possible reason: those cells were never copied from the mutated one. The word for a person in this state is a <b>mosaic</b> — two genetically different populations of cells in one body.</p>\n<p><b>And nothing is silenced, and there is no other version of the protein.</b> The mutant gene is transcribed, the overactive kinase is made, and it is overactive in every cell of the clone from the first day. There is no regulation story to find here and no second mechanism. The entire explanation is <b>how many cells</b>. At birth that is roughly one mutant among a hundred thousand blood stem cells, and the other ninety-nine thousand nine hundred and ninety-nine make essentially all of the blood.</p>\n<p>Two consequences worth stating because they follow immediately and both surprise people:</p>\n<ul><li><b>It is not passed to the patient&#x27;s children.</b> The mutation is in a blood stem cell, not in egg or sperm. What <i>is</i> inherited in some families is a variant that makes acquiring the mutation, or expanding it, a little more likely. Two different layers.</li><li><b>Where it lands decides whether it matters at all.</b> JAK2 sits under the cytokine receptors of blood cells. The identical substitution in a skin or liver stem cell would do nothing observable, which is part of why the mutation is so much commoner than the disease.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"do-they-have-the-disease-is-four-questions\">&quot;Do they have the disease?&quot; is four questions</h3>\n<p>The first version of the table below said <i>disease at birth</i> and <i>relevant cells</i> and assumed those were clear. They were not, and the terms are now defined once in <a href=\"fallakte.html#terms\"><code>../../GLOSSARY.md</code></a>. The distinction that matters:</p>\n<table><tr><th style=\"text-align:left\">state</th><th style=\"text-align:left\">the question</th></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\"><b>defect present</b></td><td style=\"text-align:left\">is the broken instruction there?</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\"><b>cells misbehaving</b></td><td style=\"text-align:left\">are the affected cells doing the wrong thing <i>now</i>?</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\"><b>detectable</b></td><td style=\"text-align:left\">would an ordinary test be abnormal?</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\"><b>harming</b></td><td style=\"text-align:left\">is the person being damaged?</td></tr></table>\n<p><b>&quot;They are born with it&quot; answers the first question only.</b> That is the sentence that makes people expect a sick baby, and it is why this needed four rows.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-two-diseases-side-by-side-with-the-cell-type-named\">The two diseases, side by side, with the cell type named</h3>\n<table><tr><th style=\"text-align:left\"></th><th style=\"text-align:left\">familial hypercholesterolaemia</th><th style=\"text-align:left\">polycythaemia vera</th></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">where the mutation is</td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><b>every cell</b>, from conception</td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><b>one blood stem cell</b>, acquired later</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">how it got there</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">inherited from a parent</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">a copying error during that person&#x27;s life, often before birth</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">the cells whose malfunction makes the phenotype</td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><b>liver cells that clear LDL from blood</b></td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><b>blood stem cells in the marrow</b></td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">what share of those cells carry it at birth</td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><b>all of them</b></td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><b>one in about a hundred thousand</b></td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">are cells misbehaving at birth</td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><b>yes, all of them.</b> Clearance is impaired immediately</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">yes, but only that one cell, which makes a thousandth of one per cent of the blood</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">is an ordinary test abnormal in childhood</td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><b>yes.</b> The cholesterol is high from infancy</td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><b>no.</b> The blood count is normal</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">what the delay before harm is made of</td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><b>accumulation.</b> The artery has to take the damage</td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><b>expansion, and then accumulation.</b> The clone has to grow first, and only then does anything start to damage the person</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">first harm, untreated</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">teens with two bad copies; fifth decade with one</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">sixth decade</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">passed to the person&#x27;s children</td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><b>yes, to about half</b></td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><b>no.</b> It is not in egg or sperm</td></tr></table>\n<h3 id=\"so-answering-the-questions-directly\">So, answering the questions directly</h3>\n<p><b>If you inherit it, do you start over-producing straight away?</b> Yes. In hypercholesterolaemia the failure to clear LDL is running from infancy and the blood test is already abnormal in a young child. <b>Nothing is waiting.</b> What takes forty years is the artery accumulating the damage, which is a different clock from the defect.</p>\n<p><b>So a germline disease has a delay too?</b> It does, and that is worth being clear about because it is where the two look alike. <b>The difference is what the delay is made of.</b> In the inherited disease the misbehaving cells are all present from the start and the <i>damage</i> accumulates. In the somatic disease the misbehaving cells are almost absent from the start and have to <i>multiply</i> before any damage can begin. <b>Polycythaemia vera carries both delays, stacked</b>, which is the real reason it presents later.</p>\n<p><b>Somatic: the mutation arrives after a functional population already exists?</b> <b>Yes. That is exactly it, and it is the whole answer to the question.</b> There is a working marrow, built normally, running normally. One of its stem cells makes a copying error. From that moment the person has two populations: a very large normal one that is making all of their blood, and a clone of one that is making almost none of it. Nothing about the normal population changed, and nothing protects it. It simply was never descended from the cell that made the error.</p>\n<p><b>And the mutation never spreads.</b> This is the point most worth holding onto. A mutation cannot move from one cell to another. The only way it gets into more cells is for the cell that has it to divide. So &quot;the clone grew&quot; always means <i>that cell&#x27;s descendants became a larger share of the marrow</i>, and never <i>the mutation got into other cells</i>.</p>\n<p>One refinement for completeness, which is a dose effect inside the clone and not an answer to the question above: the mutant cells are usually heterozygous, so even they carry one normal copy of the gene. Some clones later lose it, and a mutant fraction above half marks those patients, who carry more risk.</p>\n<p><b>2026-10-10 — and then the resolution turned out to be too simple.</b> Lineage work published in 2022 dated the mutation&#x27;s acquisition in individual patients to childhood, in some cases before birth. So the mutation frequently <i>is</i> early. What takes forty years is the clone.</p>\n<pre>the disease is not the mutation.\nthe disease is the mutation x the share of your marrow descended from it x time.</pre>\n<p><b>2026-10-10 — our own Strombild model was modelling the wrong thing, and we only noticed by arguing.</b> <code>erythropoiesis-pv.strombild</code> has the disease as one edge with a fixed constant. That is a marrow already taken over: right about the steady state, silent about the forty years, and therefore unable to express the question the case is actually about. A share and a rate is a stock and a flow, so the natural history became <code>clonal-escape.strombild</code> the same day.</p>\n<p><b>2026-10-10, second pass — the model&#x27;s own falsifier fired, in four hours.</b> Section 8 lists route R1 as &quot;measure the growth advantage in carriers&quot;, with the falsifier: <i>if growth rates in carriers and patients are indistinguishable, the escape-velocity account is wrong.</i> Checking whether anyone had the data found that they do, and that the answer is in between.</p>\n<p>A Danish general-population study screened 19,958 people and followed 67 carriers for more than ten years with serial allele fractions. Snyder and colleagues (PNAS, 2026) fitted a <b>Moran process</b> to them: a fixed stem-cell population, mutant and wild-type competing for it, one parameter for the mutant&#x27;s self-replication advantage. <b>That is the same structure as <code>clonal-escape</code>, arrived at independently, which is reassuring and is also why their result lands on us so directly.</b></p>\n<p>Three findings, and two of them hurt:</p>\n<table><tr><th style=\"text-align:left\">finding</th><th style=\"text-align:left\">what it does to our model</th></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">The advantage is measurable per person, and higher on average in those who progressed</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">supports the mechanism</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">It has <b>limited predictive value for an individual</b>. Some with a negative advantage progressed; some whose clones clearly expanded did not</td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><b>the escape-velocity account is incomplete</b>, and Q2&#x27;s answer is neither &quot;rate&quot; nor &quot;dice&quot;</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\"><b>12 of 67 clones were significantly SHRINKING</b></td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><code>clonal-escape</code> cannot produce this at all. It holds the advantage fixed for life, so a clone can only stall or grow</td></tr></table>\n<p>So the headline correction is to the method, not the biology. <b>An ask is a claim that nobody has done something, and it carries the same standing as any other claim in this project.</b> Ours was <code>alleged</code>, was never checked, and was published in this file for four hours. It has been rewritten as a calibration task and Q7 exists because of the twelve shrinking clones.</p>\n<p><b>One refinement that is easy to get backwards.</b> Inheritance does participate, one level up. A common inherited variant in the same gene raises the chance of <i>acquiring</i> the mutation, and the disease clusters in families. <b>Two layers: something inherited changes the odds of the somatic event; the somatic event plus time changes the blood.</b> Neither layer is actionable today, which is why the record rates prevention <code>none</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-does-one-cell-win-and-does-it-ever-happen-at-birth\">Why does one cell win? And does it ever happen at birth?</h3>\n<p>Both asked on 2026-10-10, and they are the same arithmetic from two ends.</p>\n<p><b>It does happen at birth, or before it.</b> The lineage-tracing work dated the mutation in the patients where <i>JAK2</i> was the first event to somewhere between 33 weeks of gestation and 10.8 years of age, and for the earliest case the interval runs from about 11 weeks after conception. So the answer to &quot;does it ever happen at birth&quot; is yes, routinely.</p>\n<p><b>And that is exactly why it does not present early.</b> One cell is one cell. The stem-cell pool that keeps an adult in blood is of the order of a hundred thousand actively contributing cells, which is the number the published model assumes. A single mutant among them is about a thousandth of one per cent of the marrow, and it produces about a thousandth of one per cent of the blood. <b>Nothing is wrong with that person&#x27;s blood count, because almost nothing about their blood comes from that cell.</b> The disease is not waiting for a second event; it is waiting for arithmetic.</p>\n<p><b>Why one cell wins at all is the part that sounds implausible, and the first thing to say is that it is not special to this disease.</b> Expanded clones from one driver or another are found in most people by their seventies. A blood system that is still one diverse population at eighty is the exception. So &quot;one cell takes over a large share of the marrow&quot; is close to the normal ageing of blood, and PV is a case where the driver that won happens to also raise the red cell count.</p>\n<p>The mechanism of the winning is compounding. Stem cells self-renew, so any inherited change that tilts renewal against differentiation even slightly is multiplied every generation of cells. The published estimates are of a self-replication advantage of the order of tens of per cent, over a forty-year run. <b>A small edge and a long time, which is the same arithmetic as interest.</b> Our model&#x27;s shipped dial corresponds to roughly a ten per cent advantage, by my own conversion from their units, and that is the number to calibrate properly.</p>\n<p>What the advantage is MADE OF is Q1 and is open. The leading candidates are that the mutant kinase signals through the thrombopoietin receptor without a ligand, which is the stem cell&#x27;s own self-renewal pathway rather than the erythropoietin receptor that produces the visible phenotype; and that chronic inflammation favours mutant stem cells over their neighbours, which would mean the clone partly manufactures its own advantage. Neither is settled.</p>\n<p><b>And it frequently does not win, which is the honest other half.</b> About one adult in thirty carries the mutation and about one in a hundred thousand per year gets the disease. Three reasons it fails, in rough order of how much they probably matter:</p>\n<ol><li><b>The mutation has to land in a cell that lives long enough.</b> Most divisions in the marrow happen in progenitors, which die with their lineage. A V617F progenitor takes the mutation to the grave in weeks. Only a hit in a long-lived stem cell persists at all.</li><li><b>A clone of one cell is easily lost by chance.</b> Early on, the numbers are small enough that the stochastic term dominates the advantage.</li><li><b>The advantage can be too small, or can reverse.</b> Twelve of the sixty-seven followed carriers were losing ground. Inherited variants affecting blood-cell traits are known to influence how strongly the clone is selected, which is a second, quieter way in which inheritance participates in a somatic disease.</li></ol>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"4-open-questions\">4. Open questions</h2>\n<p>Numbered to match <code>case.yaml</code>. Each is tagged with the Engpass obstacle it belongs to where one exists.</p>\n<p><b>Q1 · What is the renewal advantage made of?</b> Its MAGNITUDE is now measured per person. What it is made of is not. Is it a property of the mutation, of the host, or of the niche? Those are three different interventions, and the leading candidates are ligand-free signalling through the stem cell&#x27;s own self-renewal receptor and inflammation that favours the mutant over its neighbours. <i>(Engpass: <code>who-progresses</code>)</i></p>\n<p><b>Q2 · Why do most carriers never get the disease?</b> <code>partial</code>, as of the second pass on 2026-10-10. Posed as &quot;smaller advantage or unluckier dice&quot;, and the evidence says neither alone: the growth advantage is higher on average in those who progress and is a poor predictor of any individual. <b>The residual after growth rate is accounted for is now a quantified residual rather than a shrug</b>, and it is what caps the record&#x27;s <code>prognostic</code> rating and gates route R3. <i>(Engpass: <code>who-progresses</code>)</i></p>\n<p><b>Q7 · Why do some clones shrink?</b> Twelve of sixty-seven followed carriers had a significantly negative advantage. A clone that expands for years and then declines means the advantage is not a constant of the person, and something switched it. An adaptive immune response is the authors&#x27; leading suggestion. <b>This would be the first thing resembling a natural control of the clone, and therefore a direction that is not a kinase inhibitor.</b> It also breaks <code>clonal-escape</code> as written. <i>(Engpass: <code>who-progresses</code>)</i></p>\n<p><b>Q9 · Which constraint actually binds in a patient: the marrow&#x27;s capacity, or the iron supply?</b> Raised 2026-10-11 by our own two models disagreeing. In <code>erythropoiesis-pv</code> the marrow edge is the only brake, so weakening it works. In <code>iron</code> the iron supply is the only brake, so weakening the marrow does <b>nothing at any dose</b>. Real marrow has both limits and nobody measures which is binding in a given person. <b>It decides whether a kinase inhibitor, a hepcidin mimetic or a needle is the right lever for them</b>, which is a live clinical question now that all three exist, and it is answerable with existing measurements rather than new science.</p>\n<p><b>Q8 · Is V617F always the first event?</b> In some patients it is, and in others <i>TET2</i> or <i>DNMT3A</i> came first, with the order apparently mattering for which disease results. <b>If a prior mutation is what makes a <i>JAK2</i> clone able to expand, the advantage in Q1 is a property of the cell&#x27;s history rather than of the mutation</b>, and the earlier event is the target. This is step 1b of the chain.</p>\n<p><b>Q3 · One mutation, three diseases.</b> The same V617F is found in essential thrombocythaemia and primary myelofibrosis. Allele burden, homozygosity, the order in which co-mutations arrive and host factors all contribute, and none of it predicts the phenotype. The record&#x27;s <code>mechanism: established</code> has a stated case for <code>partial</code> resting on this hole alone. <i>(Engpass: <code>who-progresses</code>)</i></p>\n<p><b>Q4 · Why is the thrombosis not only viscosity?</b> Lowering the haematocrit lowers the risk and does not abolish it. This is the residual harm in the patients treatment already reaches, and <b>no model of ours covers the step.</b></p>\n<p><b>Q5 · What drives transformation to myelofibrosis or leukaemia, and can it be seen coming?</b> It is what kills the patients whose thrombosis has been controlled. <i>(Engpass: <code>who-progresses</code>)</i></p>\n<p><b>Q6 · Does lowering the mutant allele burden change what happens to a patient?</b> Interferon alfa lowers it and produces molecular remissions in some patients. Whether that means fewer transformations or a longer life rests on retrospective comparison. <b>It is the difference between treating the stock and treating the mechanism, and the record has one slot for both.</b></p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"5-models-and-data\">5. Models and data</h2>\n<h3 id=\"ours\">Ours</h3>\n<p>Each is a text file and the published ones run in a browser. <b>What each one REFUSES to claim is the more useful half</b>, so both are written out. The paths are in <code>case.yaml</code>, which checks that they resolve and that each is discussed here.</p>\n<p><b>Strangschrift <code>jak2-sites</code>.</b> Shows the real JAK2 and erythropoietin-receptor sequences with the residues that matter marked on them: V617 in the pseudokinase domain, Y1007 in the activation loop, the receptor tyrosines a mutant kinase phosphorylates. Refuses any claim about shape. It never computes a fold, and the two domains being adjacent in the folded protein is not visible in a sequence.</p>\n<p><b>Strangschrift <code>stat5-target</code>.</b> Shows a promoter carrying the motif that activated STAT5 binds. Refuses the claim that these are the targets that matter in this disease. The motif is real and the gene is invented.</p>\n<p><b>Schaltwerk <code>jak2-brake</code>.</b> Shows the restraint itself as a rule you can step: while the pseudokinase site reads <code>braked</code>, the kinase cannot phosphorylate, and nothing fires. Refuses rates. Schaltwerk has no time in it, so it says which events are possible and never how fast.</p>\n<p><b>Schaltwerk <code>jak2-v617f</code>.</b> Shows the same board with the substitution. The brake rule no longer matches, so phosphorylation fires with no erythropoietin anywhere on the board, which is the step the prose calls constitutive activation. Refuses to explain WHY the substitution loosens the restraint. That is declared, not derived from structure.</p>\n<p><b>Schaltwerk <code>jak2-stat5</code>.</b> Shows the signal continuing past the receptor: STAT5 phosphorylated, paired, and leaving. Refuses to say which genes it then turns on, which is step 6 and is open.</p>\n<p><b>Schaltwerk <code>jak2-off</code>.</b> Shows that the off switches still work. A phosphatase removes the phosphate, and SOCS1 grips the kinase&#x27;s own phosphotyrosine and takes it to be destroyed. Refuses any statement about whether the off switch can keep up, which is a rate question and therefore Strombild&#x27;s.</p>\n<p><b>Schaltwerk <code>pfcp</code>.</b> Shows the contrast case: a germline truncation of the erythropoietin receptor that deletes the site the off switch grips. Too many red cells, from a different step, present from birth. Refuses to be a model of PV. It is on the board to be told apart from PV.</p>\n<p><b>Strombild <code>erythropoiesis</code>.</b> Shows the healthy loop, settling where the textbook says and defending a bleed in three weeks. Refuses anything below the stock level. There are no cells in it, only amounts.</p>\n<p><b>Strombild <code>erythropoiesis-pv</code>.</b> Shows the disease as ONE EDGE: maturation stops reading erythropoietin, the haematocrit climbs into the sixties, and the kidney turns erythropoietin down below 1 while working perfectly. A phlebotomy event shows the sawtooth that never ends. Refuses the forty years before that steady state. It models a marrow already taken over.</p>\n<p><b>Strombild <code>clonal-escape</code>.</b> Shows the forty years, with the clone as a stock and the renewal advantage as one dial. Refuses probability: the expansion is deterministic here and stochastic in a person, so it can say whether a given advantage escapes on average and not how often it does. It also refuses to predict an individual, and says why.</p>\n<p><b>Strombild <code>iron</code>.</b> Shows the supply side: iron recycling between red cells and stores, the hepcidin gate, and what phlebotomy costs. Its unit is the trick — iron measured in haematocrit points it could build — so taking blood out takes the iron with it and needs no separate rule. Produces the lengthening phlebotomy interval, and separates a third lever from the other two. Refuses to have any brake except iron. The marrow is a catalyst in it, never consumed, which is the mirror image of <code>erythropoiesis-pv</code> where iron does not exist. It also cannot model the induction phase, for a reason that is about the language rather than the disease.</p>\n<h3 id=\"can-a-reader-actually-open-these\">Can a reader actually open these?</h3>\n<p><b>Today, none of them</b>, and the case file says so rather than implying otherwise. <code>case.yaml</code> records one of three states per model and <code>check.py</code> prints the tally:</p>\n<table><tr><th style=\"text-align:left\">state</th><th style=\"text-align:right\">count</th><th style=\"text-align:left\">what it means</th></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\"><code>live</code></td><td style=\"text-align:right\"><b>0</b></td><td style=\"text-align:left\">openable on the public site right now</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\"><code>repo</code></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">6</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">the source is public; <b>the playground on the site is a sync behind it</b></td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\"><code>private</code></td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">Schaltwerk. It exists here and nowhere a reader can reach</td></tr></table>\n<p>The six <code>repo</code> models are two commands away from being <code>live</code>: the Strangschrift and Strombild playgrounds on the site predate every model in this case file. Until then, a reader can read the text files and not run them, which is a worse version of this case file than it should be. <b>It is the first thing on the list in section 9.</b> <code>check.py</code> prints that count rather than letting it pass.</p>\n<p><code>clonal-escape</code> is the one to read first. It makes the hypothesis runnable: the stem-cell niche is a fixed pool, the clone is a stock in it, and the mutant&#x27;s renewal advantage is a single dial. Three outcomes fall out of that one number.</p>\n<table><tr><th style=\"text-align:left\">advantage</th><th style=\"text-align:left\">clone at 85</th><th style=\"text-align:left\">haematocrit</th><th style=\"text-align:left\">what that is</th></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">low</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">nothing</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">normal</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">no escape — nothing to find</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">middle</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">a few percent</td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><b>normal for life</b></td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><b>clonal haematopoiesis</b></td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">shipped value</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">most of the marrow</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">mid-sixties</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">PV, criteria met at <b>age 60</b></td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">high</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">most of the marrow</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">seventies</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">PV, criteria met at <b>age 31</b></td></tr></table>\n<p>The middle row is the finding. <i>Most people who carry V617F never get PV</i>, and the model produces them <b>without a second mechanism</b> — same mutation, same physiology, a slightly smaller advantage, and the disease runs out of lifetime. The shipped value puts the diagnostic threshold at sixty, which is the real median age at diagnosis and was not fitted to it.</p>\n<p>And the sensitivity is the warning rather than the result: doubling the dial moves first abnormality from age 74 to age 38. <b>The model cannot predict an individual&#x27;s onset, and saying so is what makes it useful</b> — it states how precisely that growth rate would have to be measured to be worth anything.</p>\n<h3 id=\"other-people-s\">Other people&#x27;s</h3>\n<p><b>What exists and is strong.</b> Randomised trial evidence on the treatment: low-dose aspirin (ECLAP, 2004), a haematocrit target below 45% against 45–50% (CYTO-PV, 2013), ruxolitinib after hydroxyurea failure (RESPONSE, 2015), and ropeginterferon in low-risk patients (Low-PV, 2021). Large modern cohorts carry survival, thrombosis at diagnosis and transformation rates. <b>None of it is in this repository yet</b>; the record&#x27;s own <code>holes</code> say every figure in it is from memory and has not been read against the papers.</p>\n<p><b>The 2022 lineage-tracing work is the most important single result for this case</b>, because it is what makes the natural history a quantitative question rather than a mystery.</p>\n<p><b>The open protein models are a supplier, not an answer.</b> Biohub publishes an open, MIT-licensed protein stack — a sequence language model, a structure predictor for complexes, and an atlas of predicted structures — which can be pointed at JAK2 and the erythropoietin receptor today. <b>It buys geometry and variant scoring. It does not buy a single step of the chain in section 2.</b> Every model listed there answers <i>what happens next</i>, which is the layer the protein stack stops below. The honest use is to cite its structures as declared facts rather than recomputing them, and the same group&#x27;s binder-design results are the right shape for a route: a named target, a method, and a measured hit rate.</p>\n<p><b>The carrier data exists, and this file said it did not.</b> Two general-population efforts, and together they are the most important external input to this case:</p>\n<table><tr><th style=\"text-align:left\"></th><th style=\"text-align:left\"></th></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">screened, Danish general-population study</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">19,958</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">carrying V617F, sensitive assay</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">3.1%, the large majority under 1% allele fraction</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">carriers above 1% followed with serial measurements</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">67, over more than ten years</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">their clones: increasing · neutral · <b>decreasing</b></td><td style=\"text-align:left\">47 · 8 · <b>12</b></td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">of those 67, diagnosed with a neoplasm during follow-up</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">37</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">a second, less sensitive cohort at median age 63</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">0.1% carrying it</td></tr></table>\n<p>Two cautions on reading that table. The 37 of 67 is <b>not</b> a population progression rate, because the 67 were selected for already having an allele fraction above 1%, which is the high end of carriage. And the gap between the two prevalence figures, 3.1% against 0.1%, is mostly the assay rather than the populations: <b>how common this mutation is depends on how hard you look.</b></p>\n<p>The modelling paper on that cohort fits a Moran process and reports a self-replication advantage per person. It is the right comparator for <code>clonal-escape</code> and the first ask is now to calibrate against it rather than to go and collect what it already holds.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"6-detection\">6. Detection</h2>\n<p><b>The test is a single blood sample and it is cheap. The problem is that the question is not asked.</b> A raised haematocrit has many commoner explanations — smoking, dehydration, lung disease, sleep apnoea, altitude, testosterone — so the result is seen, attributed, and not repeated. The record rates diagnostic measurement <code>objective</code> and the <code>access</code> number is dominated by patients never found, not by missing drugs.</p>\n<p>Two ways it is missed, and they are different problems:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Explained away.</b> The count is abnormal and a plausible cause is to hand. A <i>JAK2</i> test would settle it.</li><li><b>Masked.</b> The haematocrit is normal and the disease is present, because iron deficiency, an enlarged spleen or an expanded plasma volume hide the red-cell mass. This is how someone presents with a hepatic vein thrombosis and an unremarkable blood count.</li></ul>\n<p><b>What the window costs.</b> About a quarter of patients have a major thrombosis at or before diagnosis. Treatment started afterwards lowers the risk of the next event and gives nothing back. The record files that under <code>residue</code>, severity <code>major</code>, and it is the strongest argument in the case for working on detection rather than on therapeutics.</p>\n<p><b>What could be built.</b> Opportunistic flagging on an already-ordered full blood count is software, not science, and <code>clonal-escape</code> says where to look: a haematocrit drifting upward across repeated counts in someone&#x27;s fifties, with a <i>falling</i> erythropoietin, is the loop&#x27;s fingerprint appearing years before any threshold is crossed.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"7-treatments-and-the-distinction-that-matters\">7. Treatments, and the distinction that matters</h2>\n<p><b>Rewritten 2026-10-11.</b> This section had two categories, stock and edge. Modelling the iron supply produced a third, and then produced a warning about all three.</p>\n<p><b>The three levers.</b></p>\n<table><tr><th style=\"text-align:left\"></th><th style=\"text-align:left\">acts on</th><th style=\"text-align:left\">what that means</th></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\"><b>the stock</b></td><td style=\"text-align:left\">red cells that already exist</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">take them out. The number falls and nothing about why it rose has changed</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\"><b>the edge</b></td><td style=\"text-align:left\">the marrow&#x27;s ability to build</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">make it less able, whatever material is available</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\"><b>the substrate</b></td><td style=\"text-align:left\">the iron it builds with</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">leave the marrow alone and starve its supply</td></tr></table>\n<table><tr><th style=\"text-align:left\">treatment</th><th style=\"text-align:left\">lever</th><th style=\"text-align:left\">what it does</th></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">venesection to a haematocrit under 45%</td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><b>stock</b>, and <b>substrate</b> by accident</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">removes red cells, and the iron inside them. <b>The iron loss is not a side effect, it is the slow half of the mechanism</b></td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">low-dose aspirin</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">downstream of the stock</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">the platelet contribution to step 11</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">hydroxyurea</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">edge, bluntly</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">suppresses marrow output without distinguishing mutant from wild-type</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\"><b>rusfertide</b> (hepcidin mimetic, approved 2026-08-28)</td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><b>substrate</b></td><td style=\"text-align:left\">mimics the gate hormone, so iron stops reaching the marrow <b>while the stores stay full.</b> Functional restriction rather than true deficiency</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">ruxolitinib</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">edge, bluntly</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">inhibits JAK2, mutant and wild-type alike. Lowers allele burden modestly</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">interferon alfa</td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><b>the clone</b></td><td style=\"text-align:left\">lowers allele burden over years. Whether that changes outcomes is <b>Q6</b></td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">a mutant-selective JAK2 inhibitor</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">the edge, precisely</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">does not exist in practice. Early trials</td></tr></table>\n<h3 id=\"why-iron-is-not-a-side-effect\">Why iron is not a side effect</h3>\n<p>Venesection works twice. It removes red cells immediately, and it removes <b>iron</b>, because the iron is inside the cells. Haemoglobin is where most of the body&#x27;s iron is, and the body is a nearly closed iron loop: red cells live about four months, macrophages recover their iron, and diet contributes a small fraction of the daily throughput. So repeated venesection empties the stores, and <b>an iron-starved marrow cannot rebuild the red cell mass quickly.</b></p>\n<p>That is why maintaining the deficiency <i>is</i> the treatment, and why giving iron back undoes it. The register&#x27;s record files the deliberate deficiency under what treatment costs: fatigue, restless legs, poor concentration, uncorrected on purpose.</p>\n<p>And the body fights it. The over-driven marrow suppresses hepcidin, and iron deficiency suppresses it further, which opens the gate and raises absorption. <b>That counter-regulation is why venesection is for life rather than a course of treatment.</b></p>\n<p><code>iron.strombild</code> reproduces the clinical rhythm from this alone. Its phlebotomy interval starts at 73 days and settles at 130 as the stores empty, against the record&#x27;s own prose of &quot;every few weeks at first and every few months once stable&quot;. The lengthening is an output, not a setting.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-rusfertide-changes-and-it-is-the-comparison-that-matters\">What rusfertide changes, and it is the comparison that matters</h3>\n<p>The drug mimics hepcidin, so it closes the gate instead of draining the tank. In the model, same haematocrit, opposite iron:</p>\n<table><tr><th style=\"text-align:left\"></th><th style=\"text-align:left\">phlebotomy</th><th style=\"text-align:left\">rusfertide</th></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">haematocrit held</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">yes</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">yes</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">iron stores after 2500 days</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">11.1</td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><b>19.2</b></td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\">needles</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">for life</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">weekly injection, no venesection</td></tr></table>\n<p><b>This also closes a named hole in the disease record</b>, which says rusfertide&#x27;s regulatory status was unchecked. It was approved on 28 August 2026. Whether that moves any axis is a rating decision and belongs in Nordstern, not here.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-warning-the-model-produced-which-is-worth-more-than-the-levers\">The warning the model produced, which is worth more than the levers</h3>\n<p>In <code>iron.strombild</code>, <b>pushing on the marrow edge does nothing at any dose.</b> At a sixth of normal maturation the haematocrit, the stores and the phlebotomy interval are unchanged; the only thing that moves is iron backing up in front of a slower marrow.</p>\n<p>That is not a claim about ruxolitinib. It is a claim about models: <b>where one constraint binds, every lever is either inert or decisive, and none is therapeutic.</b> Iron is the only brake in that file, so the iron lever is nearly decisive and the marrow lever is inert. <code>erythropoiesis-pv.strombild</code> has the opposite fault, with the marrow edge as the only brake and no iron at all, so there the marrow lever works and iron cannot be asked about.</p>\n<p><b>Neither file can say which constraint binds in a real patient, and that is the question a clinician has.</b> It is now <b>Q9</b>.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"8-routes\">8. Routes</h2>\n<p>Each with a falsifier. <b>A route without a falsifier is a wish.</b></p>\n<p><b>R1 · Calibrate against the measurements that exist.</b> Rewritten 2026-10-10; it previously read &quot;measure the advantage in carriers&quot;, which had already been done twice. Map our dial onto the published per-person advantage, re-run the three regimes against their distribution, and publish where our file loses. <i>Falsifier, and it has already partly fired:</i> the published estimates show the advantage is a poor predictor of an individual&#x27;s progression, so the escape-velocity account is incomplete as an explanation of WHO becomes ill. It survives as an account of the three regimes and of why onset is late. <b>If calibration shows our shipped dial is outside the observed distribution, the age-60 result is a coincidence and should be withdrawn.</b></p>\n<p><b>R2 · Turn the model into a prediction and test it against stored cohorts.</b> Given an allele burden at one age, predict the blood count at the next. <i>Falsifier:</i> the predicted ages of crossing are no better than age alone.</p>\n<p><b>R3 · Early, low-intensity clone reduction in people who have not yet crossed.</b> <code>clonal-escape</code> is the argument: act on the slope decades before the threshold. <i>Falsifier:</i> <b>this route is blocked, not merely untested, until Q2 is answered.</b> Treating carriers means treating a large majority who were never going to progress, which is the overtreatment the register derives as a flag. The ethics follow the measurement, not the other way round.</p>\n<p><b>R4 · Mutant-selective inhibition.</b> The thing that made imatinib work for the sibling disease. In early trials. <i>Falsifier:</i> selectivity achieved and allele burden falls without a change in thrombosis or transformation, which would fold R4 back into Q6.</p>\n<p><b>R6 · Merge the two one-sided models and ask which constraint binds.</b> One file where the marrow&#x27;s capacity and the iron supply are both limits, then fit it to the thing that is already measured in every patient on treatment: the phlebotomy interval and the ferritin. <b>A patient whose interval lengthens as their ferritin falls is iron-limited. One whose interval stays short with an empty ferritin is marrow-limited</b> and is the patient a kinase inhibitor should help. <i>Falsifier:</i> the interval and the ferritin turn out to carry no information about which lever works, in which case Q9 is not answerable this cheaply and needs something measured in the marrow rather than in the blood.</p>\n<p><b>R5 · A model of step 11.</b> The thrombosis is not only viscosity and nothing here covers it. Platelets, leukocytes and endothelium in a Strombild-shaped loop would at minimum say what the residual risk after a haematocrit of 45% is made of. <i>Falsifier:</i> it reproduces nothing that the haematocrit alone does not already explain.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"9-how-to-help\">9. How to help</h2>\n<p>The file is public and the models are text. Three concrete things, in order of how much they would change:</p>\n<ol><li><b>Calibrate the model against the published carrier estimates</b> (R1). The data exists and has been modelled with the same structure we used. The work is the mapping, the re-run, and an honest account of where our file loses.</li><li><b>Rebuild <code>clonal-escape</code> with an advantage that can change over a life</b> (Q7), so a clone can expand and then decline. That is structural, not a parameter, and it needs a hypothesis for what switches it.</li><li><b>Read <code>clonal-escape</code> and try to break it.</b> It needs erythropoiesis, not programming. The constants are declared in the file and the header states what the model refuses to claim; the most useful contribution is a sentence saying which of those claims is wrong.</li><li><b>A model of the thrombosis step</b> (R5), one of four empty rows in section 2.</li></ol>\n<p>Corrections to the mechanism chain are the most valuable thing anybody can send, and section 3 is where they go. <b>A case file that is wrong in a markable place is doing its job.</b></p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"10-log\">10. Log</h2>\n<p>Append-only, newest last. <code>case.yaml</code> carries only the date of the newest entry, and <code>check.py</code> fails if that date is not found here.</p>\n<p><b>2026-10-10 · case opened.</b> First disease anyone started on, for a nonprofit partner. The opening discussion was a correction: the working assumption was the ordinary one, a genetic disease with bad code and a faulty protein, which fails the obvious test. If the mutation were inherited the disease would arrive in childhood, and it arrives at sixty. The resolution is that V617F is somatic. The germline control is familial hypercholesterolaemia, which that argument queued as a new Nordstern record. The control inside this phenotype is familial erythrocytosis, which is germline and does present in childhood. Then the lineage-tracing result made it stranger: the mutation is often acquired before birth, and the clone is what takes decades. That is a share and a rate, so it is a stock and a flow, and it became <code>clonal-escape.strombild</code> the same day. The model produced a third outcome nobody put in: a clone that escapes and never causes disease in a lifetime, which is clonal haematopoiesis and which most carriers have.</p>\n<p><b>2026-10-10 · second entry, and it corrects the first.</b> Four questions were put to the file: are we certain it is the mutation, does it ever happen at birth, why does one cell win, and do some people get it without the cell winning. Checking the last found that the measurement this file had just listed as its number one missing input <b>already existed</b>, in two published cohorts. The result is partly a refutation of our own model, in exactly the place route R1 said to look. See section 3. Q2 moved from <code>open</code> to <code>partial</code>, Q7 and Q8 were added, and the first ask became a calibration task. The lesson is about method rather than disease: <b>an ask is a claim, and it has the same standing as any other claim.</b></p>\n<p><b>2026-10-10 · third entry.</b> The spine was emptied of prose. <code>case.yaml</code> now holds ids, links, paths, dates and statuses only; everything a person would rather read lives here. <code>check.py</code> enforces it in both directions: it rejects the prose keys if they come back, and it fails if a question id or a model path in the spine is never discussed in this file. Also added: section 11, and the explanation of mosaicism in section 3, after the question <i>how can a population of cells exist that does not have the faulty protein?</i></p>\n<p><b>2026-10-11 · iron, and a question we were not expecting.</b> Asked why PV patients are not iron deficient from making all that extra blood. <b>They are, and it is worse than that: the deficiency is also the treatment.</b> Phlebotomy removes iron inside the red cells, an iron-starved marrow cannot rebuild quickly, and giving iron back undoes the treatment. Built <code>iron.strombild</code> for the supply side. Its one trick is the unit — iron measured in haematocrit points it could build — which makes phlebotomy&#x27;s iron cost fall out with no extra rule. It reproduces the clinical rhythm from iron alone: the phlebotomy interval starts at 73 days and settles at 130 as the stores empty, against the record&#x27;s own &quot;every few weeks at first and every few months once stable&quot;. It also separates a third lever, the substrate, from stock and edge, and shows the hepcidin mimetic holding the haematocrit with the stores still full. Two things came out that were not asked for. <b>Pushing on the marrow edge in that file does nothing at any dose</b>, which is a statement about models rather than about ruxolitinib: where one constraint binds, every lever is inert or decisive and none is therapeutic. That became <b>Q9</b> and route <b>R6</b>. And one bug worth recording: the release edge first read hepcidin without reading <code>stores</code>, so an empty store went on releasing iron for ever. <b>The model manufactured the material it was supposed to be running out of</b>, and produced a tidy steady phlebotomy rhythm that was pure artefact. A conservation test now guards it. Also checked and resolved: the disease record&#x27;s hole about rusfertide&#x27;s regulatory status. Approved 28 August 2026.</p>\n<p><b>2026-10-10 · fourth entry, and the correction is to our own writing.</b> The comparison table added that morning was challenged for exactly the right reason: it used <i>relevant cells</i> and <i>disease at birth</i> as though both were clear, and neither is. Both terms are now retired, <code>GLOSSARY.md</code> was written to define the vocabulary once for every case, and <code>check.py</code> rejects a case file that uses a retired term. The table was rebuilt with the cell type named on each side and with the delay before harm split into its two kinds, <b>accumulation</b> and <b>expansion</b>, which is what actually distinguishes an inherited disease from an acquired one. The question that forced it: <i>if you inherit it, do you start over-producing?</i> The answer is yes, immediately, and it had not been said anywhere in the file.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"11-sources\">11. Sources</h2>\n<p><b>Not yet held to the register&#x27;s citation standard.</b> Nordstern requires an identifier, a retrieval date and a verbatim quote for every sourced scalar, and nothing in this file has been through that. These are pointers, read on 2026-10-10, so that a reader can check the claims rather than trust them.</p>\n<ul><li><b>Mutation timing, and whether V617F is the first event.</b> Williams et al., <i>Life histories of myeloproliferative neoplasms inferred from phylogenies</i>, Nature 602:162–168 (2022). <code>doi:10.1038/s41586-021-04312-6</code>. Phylogenies from 1,013 single-cell-derived colonies across 12 patients; V617F dated to between 33 weeks of gestation and 10.8 years in the patients where it was the first driver.</li><li><b>Carrier prevalence and per-person growth advantage.</b> Snyder et al., <i>Mathematical modeling of JAK2V617F clonal expansion in a general population cohort</i>, PNAS (2026). <code>doi:10.1073/pnas.2507773123</code>. Danish General Suburban Population Study; Moran process with a stem-cell population of 10^5, fitted by approximate Bayesian computation.</li><li><b>Inherited modifiers of clonal selection.</b> Work on polygenic scores for blood-cell traits influencing selection on V617F and progression to a neoplasm. <b>Not read; cited from a secondary description.</b></li><li><b>Inflammation as a source of the advantage.</b> Preprint work on interleukin-1 favouring early expansion of <i>JAK2</i>-mutant stem cells. <b>Not read; cited from a secondary description and it is a preprint.</b></li><li><b>Rusfertide approval and the trial behind it.</b> Approved 28 August 2026 as Mimrylo, for erythrocytosis in adults with polycythaemia vera inadequately controlled on existing therapy; weekly subcutaneous, titrated to hold the haematocrit below 45%. Phase 3 VERIFY, 293 patients, clinical response 76.9% against 32.9% on placebo added to standard of care, with phlebotomy need more than halved; commonest adverse events injection-site reactions and <b>anaemia</b>, which is the dose-limiting toxicity the model&#x27;s narrow window reflects. Read 2026-10-11 from secondary coverage, not from the trial report.</li><li><b>Protein structure and variant scoring.</b> The Biohub open protein stack, at <code>biohub.org/ai-models</code>. Relevant to geometry and to binder design, and to no step of section 2.</li></ul>\n<p><b>Two of the five above are marked as not read</b>, which is the point of having the list. The trial evidence named in section 7 is from memory and is in the same state as the disease record&#x27;s own, which says so in its <code>holes</code>.</p>","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Polycythaemia vera — case file","anchor":"polycythaemia-vera-case-file"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Where it stands","anchor":"1-where-it-stands"},{"level":2,"text":"2. The mechanism, step by step","anchor":"2-the-mechanism-step-by-step"},{"level":3,"text":"How certain is step 1?","anchor":"how-certain-is-step-1"},{"level":2,"text":"3. What we got wrong","anchor":"3-what-we-got-wrong"},{"level":3,"text":"\"Born with it\" is not the same as \"inherited\"","anchor":"born-with-it-is-not-the-same-as-inherited"},{"level":3,"text":"\"Do they have the disease?\" is four questions","anchor":"do-they-have-the-disease-is-four-questions"},{"level":3,"text":"The two diseases, side by side, with the cell type named","anchor":"the-two-diseases-side-by-side-with-the-cell-type-named"},{"level":3,"text":"So, answering the questions directly","anchor":"so-answering-the-questions-directly"},{"level":3,"text":"Why does one cell win? And does it ever happen at birth?","anchor":"why-does-one-cell-win-and-does-it-ever-happen-at-birth"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Open questions","anchor":"4-open-questions"},{"level":2,"text":"5. Models and data","anchor":"5-models-and-data"},{"level":3,"text":"Ours","anchor":"ours"},{"level":3,"text":"Can a reader actually open these?","anchor":"can-a-reader-actually-open-these"},{"level":3,"text":"Other people's","anchor":"other-people-s"},{"level":2,"text":"6. Detection","anchor":"6-detection"},{"level":2,"text":"7. Treatments, and the distinction that matters","anchor":"7-treatments-and-the-distinction-that-matters"},{"level":3,"text":"Why iron is not a side effect","anchor":"why-iron-is-not-a-side-effect"},{"level":3,"text":"What rusfertide changes, and it is the comparison that matters","anchor":"what-rusfertide-changes-and-it-is-the-comparison-that-matters"},{"level":3,"text":"The warning the model produced, which is worth more than the levers","anchor":"the-warning-the-model-produced-which-is-worth-more-than-the-levers"},{"level":2,"text":"8. Routes","anchor":"8-routes"},{"level":2,"text":"9. How to help","anchor":"9-how-to-help"},{"level":2,"text":"10. Log","anchor":"10-log"},{"level":2,"text":"11. Sources","anchor":"11-sources"}]}}],"about":{"readme":{"html":"<h1 id=\"fallakte-a-register-of-case-files\">Fallakte — a register of case files</h1>\n<p><i>German for a <b>case file</b> — the folder a case worker keeps open on one subject. Started 2026-10-10 with <code>polycythaemia-vera</code>, the first disease anyone actually began working on.</i></p>\n<p><b>The name is provisional.</b> <code>FND-C-NNNN</code> identifiers are not: an id is permanent and is what an outside citation resolves against, the same rule that holds for Nordstern&#x27;s <code>FND-D</code>/<code>FND-A</code> and Engpass&#x27;s <code>FND-O</code>. Renaming the directory is cheap for as long as nothing has been cited; renumbering is never cheap.</p>\n<p>Nordstern asks, per disease, <b>do we understand it</b> and <b>can we do anything about it</b>. Engpass asks <b>what is in the way</b>. Fallakte asks the fourth question, and it is the only one of the four that is about us:</p>\n<blockquote><b>What are we actually doing about this one, what have we found, and what do we still not know?</b></blockquote>\n<pre>python3 check.py          # validate every case file and its links\npython3 check.py --json   # the cases, machine-readable\npython3 test_boundary.py  # asserts Nordstern and Engpass still do not know this exists</pre>\n<h2 id=\"why-it-is-a-third-register-and-not-more-fields-on-the-record\">Why it is a third register and not more fields on the record</h2>\n<p><b>Because a Nordstern record is a verdict and a case file is a workspace.</b></p>\n<p>A record says where a disease stands, rated on a date, by somebody who signed the assertion, and it is append-only so that a citation keeps resolving. It is deliberately hard to change. A case file is the opposite thing: notes that grow weekly, half-answered questions, models that turn out to be wrong, a log, and a list of things a stranger could pick up. Putting the second inside the first would either bloat the verdict or freeze the workspace, and the first failure is the dangerous one — a record that changes every week is not citable.</p>\n<p>So the direction of reference is fixed and one-way:</p>\n<ol><li><b>A case file points at a record. A record never points at a case file.</b> Nordstern does not read this directory, does not validate it, and does not know it exists. <code>test_boundary.py</code> asserts that rather than trusting it. This is the same rule Engpass lives under, for the same reason: the register has to stand alone.</li><li><b>A case file never re-states a rating.</b> Where it needs the axes it cites the record. If the case file disagrees with the record, that disagreement is a finding and belongs in the log — and then in a re-rating, in Nordstern, with a new <code>FND-A</code> number.</li><li><b>Most diseases will never have one</b>, and that is the signal. A case file exists where work has started. The register holds 146 records; this directory holds the ones somebody is carrying.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"what-a-case-file-is-for\">What a case file is for</h2>\n<p>Two readers, and they want opposite things.</p>\n<p><b>Someone who might help.</b> They arrive from a search, know nothing, and need the mechanism, the open questions, and a concrete ask, in that order, in ten minutes.</p>\n<p><b>Someone who knows the disease better than we do</b> — a clinician, a researcher, the nonprofit we are working with. They will find our errors. The file is built to make that easy: <b>every step of a mechanism is marked for whether it is established, partial, or open, and every model says what it refuses to claim.</b> A case file that is wrong in a markable place is doing its job. A case file whose confident prose hides which half is guessed is not.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-vocabulary-rule\">The vocabulary rule</h2>\n<p><b>A term defined loosely is worse than a term left out</b>, and the first case file proved it inside a day. Its comparison table used <i>relevant cells</i> and <i>disease at birth</i> as though both were obvious. Neither is. <i>Relevant to what?</i> hid the fact that the answer is a different cell type per disease, and <i>disease at birth</i> collapsed four separate questions — is the defect present, are the cells misbehaving, would a test show it, is the person being harmed — which for most genetic conditions have different answers.</p>\n<p>So <code>GLOSSARY.md</code> defines the terms <b>once, for every case</b>, and each entry says what the term does NOT mean, because in this vocabulary the near-miss is the problem rather than the blank. A case file uses those definitions or adds to the glossary; it does not redefine a term locally. Retired terms are listed there and <code>check.py</code> <b>rejects a case file that uses one</b>, while allowing it to be named in quotes, so the explanation of the retirement does not trip the guard that exists because of it.</p>\n<p><b>The reader this is for is somebody new to the field.</b> They are the person most likely to notice that a sentence does not actually say anything, and the least likely to say so.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-rule-the-whole-thing-rests-on\">The rule the whole thing rests on</h2>\n<p><b>&quot;This happens, then this.&quot;</b> Prose about one thing promoting another and another thing tamping it down reads as understanding and cannot be checked. A case file&#x27;s mechanism section is therefore a numbered chain, one step per line, and each step carries three things: what changes, whether we know it, and where you can watch it happen.</p>\n<p>Where a step has no model, the file says so and leaves the column empty. <b>An empty column is the most useful thing in the file</b> — it is a piece of work somebody could do this month.</p>\n<h2 id=\"layout\">Layout</h2>\n<pre>Fallakte/\nREADME.md                  this\nTEMPLATE.md                the base template, and what each section is for\nGLOSSARY.md                terms, defined once, for every case\ncheck.py                   validation, and --build\nrender.py                  markdown -&gt; HTML, for the subset the cases use\ntest_boundary.py           the one-way dependency, asserted\ntest_check.py              link rewriting, which only matters once public\ntest_render.py             the renderer, and that it cannot inject HTML\ncases/&lt;slug&gt;/\ncase.yaml                the spine: ids, links, paths, dates, statuses\nREADME.md                the case file itself, and all of its prose\n\n../web/                      written by --build, published with the register\nfallakte-data.js           the artifact: spines + rendered documents\nfallakte.html              the landing page — the list of open cases\ncase.html                  the viewer — ?case=&lt;slug&gt;\nfallakte.css               shared by both, because they are one kind of page\nfallakte.test.js           run by `node --test web/` with the others</pre>\n<h2 id=\"publishing\">Publishing</h2>\n<pre>python3 check.py --build        # writes ../web/fallakte-data.js\npython3 test_render.py &amp;&amp; python3 test_check.py &amp;&amp; python3 test_boundary.py\nnode --test ../web/             # the page tests run with Nordstern&#x27;s\n../serve.sh                     # then open /web/fallakte.html</pre>\n<p>The pages are <b>pre-rendered and script-tagged</b>, which is Nordstern&#x27;s own rule for <code>web/</code>: no <code>fetch()</code>, so every page also opens over <code>file://</code> and a case file can be sent to somebody as a folder. The markdown is rendered by <code>render.py</code> at build time, so the browser loads no library. <b>The renderer is strict</b> — syntax it does not know is an error, not something passed through to be printed at a reader as <code>**bold**</code> — and <b>links are rewritten</b>, because a case file links to its neighbours the way a file does and those paths are dead once published.</p>\n<p>To put it on the site: commit and push <code>web/</code>, then in <code>site/frontend</code> run <code>./sync-projects.sh</code> (it vendors from GitHub, so the push comes first), <code>node build.js</code>, <code>npm run sync</code>, and <code>./clear-cache.sh</code> for the CloudFront invalidation. It lands under <code>projects/nordstern/app/</code>.</p>\n<p><code>case.yaml</code> holds what a machine should check — that the disease exists in Nordstern, that every Engpass slug resolves, that every model path resolves, that every question has an id something can cite. <code>README.md</code> holds everything a person should read. Nothing is stored in both.</p>\n<p>Working notes, reading, and anything not ready to be read by a stranger stay in <code>private/</code>, which is gitignored. <b>The repo is the artifact, not the ramblings.</b></p>","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Fallakte — a register of case files","anchor":"fallakte-a-register-of-case-files"},{"level":2,"text":"Why it is a third register and not more fields on the record","anchor":"why-it-is-a-third-register-and-not-more-fields-on-the-record"},{"level":2,"text":"What a case file is for","anchor":"what-a-case-file-is-for"},{"level":2,"text":"The vocabulary rule","anchor":"the-vocabulary-rule"},{"level":2,"text":"The rule the whole thing rests on","anchor":"the-rule-the-whole-thing-rests-on"},{"level":2,"text":"Layout","anchor":"layout"},{"level":2,"text":"Publishing","anchor":"publishing"}]},"glossary":{"html":"<h1 id=\"terms-defined-once\">Terms, defined once</h1>\n<p>Written 2026-10-10, after a table in the first case file used <i>relevant cells</i> and <i>disease at birth</i> as though they were self-explanatory. They are not, and <b>the reader coming into the field is the one the register is for.</b></p>\n<p>Every entry says what the term means <b>and what it does not mean</b>, because in this vocabulary the near-miss is the problem rather than the blank.</p>\n<p><b>One rule for case files: if a term is in this list, use it as defined here. If a case needs a term that is not here, add it here rather than redefining it locally.</b></p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"the-four-states-a-disease-can-be-in-and-they-are-not-the-same-question\">The four states a disease can be in, and they are not the same question</h2>\n<p>This is the distinction that caused the trouble. &quot;Does the baby have the disease?&quot; is four questions, and for most genetic conditions the answers differ.</p>\n<table><tr><th style=\"text-align:left\">state</th><th style=\"text-align:left\">the question</th><th style=\"text-align:left\">how you would know</th></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\"><b>defect present</b></td><td style=\"text-align:left\">is the broken instruction there?</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">sequence the gene</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\"><b>cells misbehaving</b></td><td style=\"text-align:left\">are the affected cells doing the wrong thing <i>now</i>?</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">measure what they make or clear</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\"><b>detectable</b></td><td style=\"text-align:left\">would an ordinary test on this person be abnormal?</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">run the test</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\"><b>harming</b></td><td style=\"text-align:left\">is the person being damaged?</td><td style=\"text-align:left\">look for the damage</td></tr></table>\n<p><b>They come apart in both directions.</b> A person can have the defect present and nothing misbehaving, because too few of their cells carry it. A person can have cells misbehaving and nothing detectable, for the same reason. And a person can have everything detectable for forty years with nothing harmful yet, because the harm is damage that accumulates.</p>\n<p><b>So &quot;they are born with it&quot; is an answer to the first question only</b>, and it is the sentence that makes people expect a sick baby.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"where-a-mutation-comes-from\">Where a mutation comes from</h2>\n<p><b>germline</b> — the mutation is in the sperm or egg the person was made from, so <b>it is in every cell of their body</b>, and it was there before there was a body. Inherited from a parent, and passed to about half their children. <i>It does not mean</i> the disease is present at birth. It means the <i>defect</i> is.</p>\n<p><b>somatic</b> — the mutation happens in <b>one ordinary cell of a body that already exists</b>, while that cell copies its own DNA. It is in that cell and in every cell descended from it, and <b>in nothing else.</b> Not inherited and not passed to children. <i>It does not mean</i> &quot;later in life&quot;. A somatic mutation can happen in a fetus, and in polycythaemia vera it often does. <b>Somatic is about WHICH CELLS, not WHEN.</b></p>\n<p><b>mosaic</b> — the state of a person who has a somatic mutation: <b>two genetically different populations of cells in one body.</b> The normal population is not repaired or protected. It simply was never copied from the mutated cell.</p>\n<p><b>clone</b> — one cell and all of its descendants. When a case file says <i>the clone grew</i>, it means that cell&#x27;s share of a tissue increased, not that the mutation spread. <b>A mutation never spreads between cells. Only descent carries it.</b></p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"how-much-of-a-tissue-the-clone-is\">How much of a tissue the clone is</h2>\n<p><b>relevant cells</b> — <i>retired. Do not use.</i> It was shorthand for &quot;the cells whose malfunction produces this phenotype&quot;, and it hid the fact that the answer differs per disease. <b>Name the cell type.</b> For familial hypercholesterolaemia it is the liver cells that clear LDL from blood. For polycythaemia vera it is the blood stem cells in the marrow.</p>\n<p><b>clonal fraction</b> — the share of a tissue descended from the mutated cell. This is the quantity that turns a mutation into a disease, and it is why a case file can need a stock-and-flow model to say anything at all.</p>\n<p><b>allele burden</b> / <b>variant allele fraction (VAF)</b> — what is actually measured in a blood sample: the fraction of gene copies carrying the mutation. <b>Not the same as clonal fraction.</b> Each cell has two copies of the gene and usually only one is mutated, so a clone that is 60% of the blood reads as about 30% allele burden. A reading above half means some cells have lost their good copy.</p>\n<p><b>clonal haematopoiesis</b> — a detectable clone in the blood with no disease. Common with age, and <b>the ordinary outcome of carrying one of these mutations</b> rather than a rare near-miss.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"why-the-harm-is-late-and-the-two-reasons-are-different\">Why the harm is late, and the two reasons are different</h2>\n<p><b>Both of these are delays between a defect and a harm, and confusing them is what makes inherited and acquired disease look alike.</b></p>\n<p><b>accumulation delay</b> — the cells misbehave from the start and the <i>damage</i> takes decades to add up. Familial hypercholesterolaemia is this: cholesterol clearance is impaired from infancy, the blood test is abnormal in childhood, and what takes until the fifth decade is the artery.</p>\n<p><b>expansion delay</b> — the cells that misbehave are too few to matter, and what takes decades is <b>the clone growing</b>. Polycythaemia vera has this delay, and then the accumulation delay on top of it. <b>Two delays stacked</b>, which is why it presents later than a germline disease of comparable severity.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"iron-because-it-turns-out-to-be-the-material-and-not-a-detail\">Iron, because it turns out to be the material and not a detail</h2>\n<p><b>haem iron</b> — most of the iron in a body is inside haemoglobin, in circulating red cells. <b>So blood is where the iron is</b>, and losing blood is losing iron. This is the fact that makes venesection do two things at once.</p>\n<p><b>the iron loop</b> — iron is <b>recycled, not consumed.</b> A red cell lives about four months, a macrophage eats it and recovers its iron, and the iron is used again. Diet contributes a small fraction of the daily throughput. <b>The body is a nearly closed loop with a small leak</b>, which is why repeated blood loss is expensive and why there is no quick way to replace it.</p>\n<p><b>hepcidin</b> — the hormone that controls whether iron is <i>available</i>. High hepcidin locks iron away. It is made in proportion to how loaded the iron stores are, and removed in proportion to how hard the marrow is demanding iron. <i>It does not mean</i> an iron transporter. Hepcidin is the signal; ferroportin is the door.</p>\n<p><b>ferroportin</b> — the pore iron leaves a cell through, on macrophages and gut lining. Hepcidin&#x27;s whole job is to have it destroyed. <b>No ferroportin, no iron released</b>, however full the stores are.</p>\n<p><b>erythroferrone</b> — the signal an over-driven marrow sends to suppress hepcidin, so that demand opens the gate. It is why a marrow making too many red cells gets more iron rather than less.</p>\n<p><b>absolute iron deficiency</b> — the stores are empty. There is no iron.</p>\n<p><b>functional iron deficiency</b> — <b>the stores are full and the iron cannot get out.</b> A closed gate rather than an empty tank. The distinction matters because it is the difference between what venesection does and what a hepcidin mimetic does, and the second holds the haematocrit without stripping the patient.</p>\n<p><b>ferritin</b> — the blood test that estimates how much iron is in the stores. Low ferritin is the usual marker of absolute deficiency.</p>\n<h2 id=\"on-this-disease-specifically\">On this disease specifically</h2>\n<p><b>polycythaemia</b> — too many red blood cells. A description, not a cause.</p>\n<p><b>polycythaemia vera</b> — the version caused by a clone in the marrow that makes red cells without being told to. <i>Vera</i> means &quot;true&quot;, which in practice distinguishes it from the far commoner <b>secondary</b> polycythaemia, where the marrow is normal and is responding correctly to a real signal, as at altitude or in lung disease.</p>\n<p><b>erythropoietin (EPO)</b> — the hormone the kidney releases to tell the marrow to make more red cells. <b>In this disease it is low</b>, because the kidney is working and the marrow has stopped listening.</p>\n<p><b>haematocrit</b> — the percentage of blood volume that is red cells. The number treatment is aimed at.</p>\n<p><b>myeloproliferative neoplasm</b> — the family this disease belongs to: a blood cancer in which the marrow overproduces mature cells rather than failing to make them.</p>","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Terms, defined once","anchor":"terms-defined-once"},{"level":2,"text":"The four states a disease can be in, and they are not the same question","anchor":"the-four-states-a-disease-can-be-in-and-they-are-not-the-same-question"},{"level":2,"text":"Where a mutation comes from","anchor":"where-a-mutation-comes-from"},{"level":2,"text":"How much of a tissue the clone is","anchor":"how-much-of-a-tissue-the-clone-is"},{"level":2,"text":"Why the harm is late, and the two reasons are different","anchor":"why-the-harm-is-late-and-the-two-reasons-are-different"},{"level":2,"text":"Iron, because it turns out to be the material and not a detail","anchor":"iron-because-it-turns-out-to-be-the-material-and-not-a-detail"},{"level":2,"text":"On this disease specifically","anchor":"on-this-disease-specifically"}]},"template":{"html":"<h1 id=\"the-case-file-template\">The case file template</h1>\n<p>Copy <code>cases/polycythaemia-vera/</code> and replace. Sections are in this order because it is the order a stranger needs them in. <b>A section with nothing in it stays, holding the sentence that says so</b> — an empty section is a statement about the field, and deleting it makes the file look finished when it is not.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"the-split-and-it-is-enforced\">The split, and it is enforced</h2>\n<p><b><code>case.yaml</code> holds nothing a person would rather read.</b> Ids, links, paths, dates and statuses: the things a checker can verify. <b><code>README.md</code> holds everything else.</b> Nothing is stored in both.</p>\n<p>The first version of this register put a model&#x27;s <code>shows</code>/<code>refuses</code>, each question&#x27;s text, the asks and the whole log inside the YAML, where prose goes to die in an eighty-column gutter. <code>check.py</code> now enforces the split <b>in both directions</b>:</p>\n<ul><li>It <b>rejects</b> the prose keys if they come back (<code>shows</code>, <code>refuses</code>, <code>ask</code>, <code>would_move</code>, <code>asks</code>, <code>log</code>, <code>routes</code>, <code>role</code>, <code>what</code>), naming the README section each belongs in.</li><li>It <b>fails</b> if a question id or a model path in the spine is never discussed in the README. <i>A question with a status and no text is a number, not a question. A model listed with no prose is a citation, not a case file.</i></li><li>It <b>fails</b> if the README carries no entry dated <code>updated</code>. The log lives in the README; the spine points at its newest date.</li></ul>\n<p><code>one_line</code> is the single exception, capped at a sentence, because a register listing needs something to show.</p>\n<h2 id=\"case-yaml-the-spine\"><code>case.yaml</code> — the spine</h2>\n<pre>slug: &lt;same as the Nordstern slug&gt;\nid: FND-C-0001              # PERMANENT. Next number, never reused.\ndisease: &lt;nordstern slug&gt;   # must resolve in Nordstern&#x27;s built artifact\ndisease_id: FND-D-0145      # must match that record&#x27;s id\nopened: 2026-10-10\nupdated: 2026-10-10         # the newest log date, which must appear in README\nstatus: active              # active | dormant | closed\n\none_line: &gt;\n  Why this case is open, in one sentence a register listing can print.\n\nworking_with:\n  - kind: nonprofit         # nonprofit | clinic | academic | patient-org | company | individual\n    named: false            # has this partner agreed to appear in a public repo?\n\nobstacles: [who-progresses] # Engpass slugs this case is working on\n\nmodels:                     # OUR artifacts. Paths are checked; so is discussion.\n  - tool: Strombild\n    ref: ../../../../strombild/examples/clonal-escape.strombild\n    access: repo            # live | repo | private  — see below\n    # live: https://...     # REQUIRED when access is `live`, forbidden otherwise\n\nquestions:                  # the id and its state; the question itself is prose\n  - id: Q1\n    status: open            # open | partial | answered | abandoned\n    obstacles: [who-progresses]</pre>\n<p><b><code>access</code> IS NOT A BOOLEAN, and it used to be.</b> <code>published: true</code> turned out to mean three different things at once: the repo is public, the playground on the site is current, and a reader can click it. Those came apart immediately — the site&#x27;s playgrounds were a sync behind every model in the first case file, so a boolean would have told readers they could open eleven things they could not open any of.</p>\n<table><tr><th style=\"text-align:left\"><code>access</code></th><th style=\"text-align:left\">means</th></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\"><code>live</code></td><td style=\"text-align:left\">openable on the public site today. <b><code>live:</code> must give the URL</b></td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\"><code>repo</code></td><td style=\"text-align:left\">the source is public; the playground on the site is behind it</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\"><code>private</code></td><td style=\"text-align:left\">neither. It exists in-tree and nowhere a reader can reach</td></tr></table>\n<p>A <code>live:</code> URL on anything but <code>live</code> is an error, because <b>a link a reader cannot follow is worse than no link.</b></p>\n<p><b>BLOCK MAPPINGS ONLY.</b> Nordstern&#x27;s bounded loader does not read inline flow mappings (<code>{tool: x, ref: y}</code>) and hands them back as strings. It does not complain, and the checker then sees ten strings where it expected ten mappings.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"readme-md-the-sections-and-what-each-is-for\"><code>README.md</code> — the sections, and what each is for</h2>\n<p><b>0. Terms.</b> Link <code>GLOSSARY.md</code> and define only what is specific to this disease. <b>Do not redefine a shared term locally.</b> If the case needs a term the glossary lacks, add it to the glossary. <code>check.py</code> warns on a case file that never links the glossary, and errors on one that uses a retired term.</p>\n<p><b>1. Where it stands.</b> Three or four lines, then the citation. Capability, delivery, and the blockers, read off the Nordstern record, <b>never re-rated here</b>. A reader has to know whether this is an unsolved mechanism or an undelivered cure before anything else makes sense.</p>\n<p><b>2. The mechanism, step by step.</b> The heart of the file, and the reason the register exists. A numbered chain, one step per row, each carrying:</p>\n<table><tr><th style=\"text-align:left\"></th><th style=\"text-align:left\"></th></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\"><b>what changes</b></td><td style=\"text-align:left\">the actual event, in the smallest terms that are true</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\"><b>status</b></td><td style=\"text-align:left\"><code>established</code> · <code>partial</code> · <code>open</code> — the same vocabulary as the record&#x27;s <code>mechanism</code> axis, applied <i>per step</i></td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align:left\"><b>where you can watch it</b></td><td style=\"text-align:left\">the model, or empty</td></tr></table>\n<p>Two rules. <b>No step may be a verb like &quot;promotes&quot; or &quot;modulates&quot; standing alone</b> — say which thing moves where, or mark the step <code>open</code>. And <b>an empty model column is left empty</b>, because that is the list of things worth building.</p>\n<p><b>3. What we got wrong.</b> Dated, kept, never edited away. The file&#x27;s credibility rests on this section more than on any other.</p>\n<p><b>4. Open questions.</b> Numbered to match <code>case.yaml</code>, each with what an answer would change, and each pointing at an Engpass obstacle where one exists. A question with no obstacle is either new, or not really an open question.</p>\n<p><b>5. Models and data.</b> Ours, by tool, each with what it shows and what it refuses. Then other people&#x27;s: datasets, cohorts, registries, published models, and the tools that can be pointed at this disease. <b>Say what each one buys and what it does not.</b> A protein structure predictor buys geometry; it does not buy mechanism, and a case file that lists it without saying so is misleading.</p>\n<p><b>6. Detection.</b> How it is found, how it is missed, what the miss costs, and what could be built. The most common thing a case file discovers is that the test exists and the question is not asked.</p>\n<p><b>7. Treatments.</b> The current ladder. For each, the one distinction that has turned out to matter: <b>does it act on the stock or on the edge?</b> Taking blood out lowers a number and changes nothing about why the number rose. Lowering the clone changes the mechanism. A ladder that does not separate those two is describing care, not strategy.</p>\n<p><b>8. Routes.</b> Candidate interventions, each with a falsifier. <b>A route without a falsifier is a wish</b> — write what result would kill it, or do not write it.</p>\n<p><b>9. How to help.</b> The <code>asks</code> from the spine, in prose, with a way to make contact.</p>\n<p><b>10. Log.</b> Dated, append-only. What happened and what changed.</p>\n<p><b>11. Sources.</b> Pointers with resolvable identifiers, and <b>a mark on every one that has not actually been read.</b> Nordstern requires an identifier, a date and a verbatim quote for a sourced scalar; a case file is not held to that, so the compensating rule is that an unread source says so. A list in which everything looks equally solid is worse than no list.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"the-rule-that-cost-the-most-to-learn\">The rule that cost the most to learn</h2>\n<p><b>AN ASK IS A CLAIM.</b> &quot;Nobody has measured this&quot; has exactly the same standing as any other assertion in this project, and Engpass&#x27;s vocabulary applies to it: <code>alleged</code> until checked. The first case file published an ask for data that already existed in two published cohorts, and it survived four hours.</p>\n<p>So before writing anything into <code>asks</code>, search for it. And when the search finds that it was done, <b>the correction goes in section 3 with its date</b> rather than quietly replacing the ask, because the mistake is more instructive than the fix.</p>","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"The case file template","anchor":"the-case-file-template"},{"level":2,"text":"The split, and it is enforced","anchor":"the-split-and-it-is-enforced"},{"level":2,"text":"case.yaml — the spine","anchor":"case-yaml-the-spine"},{"level":2,"text":"README.md — the sections, and what each is for","anchor":"readme-md-the-sections-and-what-each-is-for"},{"level":2,"text":"The rule that cost the most to learn","anchor":"the-rule-that-cost-the-most-to-learn"}]}}};
